  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <h1>Tiger Walk</h1>
    <p><em>By Daniel Cowen</em></p>

    <p>This is the personal context for why this site exists. It is not a universal prescription. What helped me may not help you. But it explains the lens through which this resource was built.</p>

    <h2>Inciting injury (2015)</h2>
    <p>In 2015 I was injured snowboarding. After that fall, a new pattern entered my body.</p>

    <p>Every few months I would be hit by severe SI pain, just to the right of my coccyx. The pain could be so intense that I could not walk. It would last for weeks and then disappear completely.</p>

    <p>Sometimes the pain radiated down my leg like sciatica. I would try massaging everything: glutes, hamstrings, calves. Nothing helped.</p>

    <p>The unpredictability was the worst part. Sometimes there was a clear trigger. Sometimes there was none. I could never tell when it was coming.</p>

    <p>From the beginning, this was not soreness. It was collapse.</p>

    <h2>Years of failed approaches (2015–2021)</h2>
    <p>I tried everything people usually try:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Acupuncture</li>
      <li>Martial arts bodywork</li>
      <li>Physical therapy</li>
      <li>Massage</li>
      <li>Mobility work</li>
      <li>Self-release</li>
      <li>Medication</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Some things helped temporarily. Nothing lasted.</p>

    <p>No one could explain the mechanics. No one could tell me what the joint needed to stay stable under load. I was given treatments, not a model.</p>

    <p>The cycle became familiar:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Pain would disappear for months.</li>
      <li>Then come roaring back for weeks.</li>
      <li>I would brace constantly.</li>
      <li>I would need medication.</li>
      <li>Then it would fade again.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>I lived in baseline tension. Always guarding. Always afraid of the next flare.</p>

    <p>I did not trust my body.</p>

    <h2>Cycling reveals asymmetry (2021–2022)</h2>
    <p>In 2021 I started cycling seriously.</p>

    <p>In 2022 a bike fit revealed a congenital leg length discrepancy. I added shoe shims to my bike shoes and daily shoes. This helped, but it created a new pattern.</p>

    <p>I was stabilizing with external correction and internal bracing. I felt supported, but fragile.</p>

    <p>The flare cycle continued:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Months of calm.</li>
      <li>Then weeks of collapse.</li>
      <li>Then bracing and meds again.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>At this point I realized something important:<br>
    Compensations are dynamic. The system keeps rerouting.</p>

    <p>There is no local fix for a global stabilization problem.</p>

    <h2>Strength without coordination (2023)</h2>
    <p>A physical therapist prescribed glute strengthening.</p>

    <p>It helped a bit.<br>
    But nothing stuck.</p>

    <p>I was building muscle without reorganizing gait. I had strength, but not control under load. The pelvis still collapsed in stance.</p>

    <p>The SI joint was still being asked to stabilize instead of transmit force.</p>

    <h2>The Florida clue (early 2025)</h2>
    <p>A few days before everything changed, I went running in Florida. It was sunny. It felt great.</p>

    <p>But afterward my quads were destroyed. Burned far beyond normal fatigue.</p>

    <p>That was the clue.</p>

    <p>My quads were doing propulsion and lateral stabilization. My glutes were offline. My pelvis was collapsing every step.</p>

    <p>At the same time I realized:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>I work from home.</li>
      <li>I sit most of the day.</li>
      <li>My glutes rarely activate.</li>
      <li>My system lives in hip flexion.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>I had forgotten how to stand.</p>

    <h2>The breakthrough: Tiger Walk</h2>
    <p>I started pressure-testing the mechanics.</p>

    <p>One night I discovered posterior-chain driven walking. Shortened stride. Glute-led pullback. Tall posture. No bracing. No pushing.</p>

    <p>The moment I found it, my vision sharpened. My perception changed. My body felt predatory. Stable. Organized.</p>

    <p>Later a PT confirmed the connection: glute med engagement affects hyoid position and visual stabilization. This is part of the predator gait circuit.</p>

    <p>I named it Tiger Walk.</p>

    <p>Not as a metaphor.<br>
    As a description of the pattern.</p>

    <p>From that moment:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Guarding dropped.</li>
      <li>Tension released.</li>
      <li>Pain quieted.</li>
      <li>Fear disappeared.</li>
      <li>Stability returned.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>My SI was no longer stabilizing. It returned to its real job: load transmission.</p>

    <h2>The key insight: control, not alignment</h2>
    <p>My pelvis was not misaligned.</p>

    <p>It was uncontrolled.</p>

    <p>Alignment fixes felt good because they reduced tone. But they did not build capacity. Stretching felt good because it reduced tension. But the problem was not tightness. It was coordination and timing.</p>

    <p>The failure mode was stance collapse under fatigue.</p>

    <p>The solution was restoring the original load path.</p>

    <h2>Flares and multipliers</h2>
    <p>Looking back, the worst flares followed combinations of:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Running without stabilization</li>
      <li>Poor sleep</li>
      <li>Emotional stress</li>
      <li>Snowboarding</li>
      <li>High load without recovery</li>
    </ul>

    <p>All share one feature: stabilizer fatigue.</p>

    <p>When the glute med drops offline, the SI catches shear.</p>

    <p>The joint is innocent. The control loop is not.</p>

    <h2>Where I am now</h2>
    <p>I trust my body again because I understand the mechanism.</p>

    <p>When I feel a twinge, I do not panic. I reorganize. I know how to stabilize and realign.</p>

    <p>Running is not the problem.<br>
    Fatigue without control is.</p>

    <p>Sitting is not neutral.<br>
    It erases gait.</p>

    <p>Walking is now training, not transport.</p>

    <p>Tiger Walk is not rehab.<br>
    It is restoration.</p>

    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>SI pain is confusing. Standard advice often treats it as a joint problem, a mobility problem, or an alignment problem.</p>

    <p>I spent nearly a decade chasing fixes that did not match my failure mode.</p>

    <p>This site exists to share the framework that finally worked for me: control under load.</p>

    <p>Not as marketing.<br>
    Not as a course.<br>
    Not as a miracle cure.</p>

    <p>Just as a public resource for people stuck in the same loop I was.</p>

    <p>If it helps, use it.<br>
    If it does not, discard it and find what works for you.</p>

    <h2>Feedback welcome</h2>
    <p>If this approach helps you, or if something is unclear or incomplete, use the <a href="/feedback">Feedback form</a>. Responses help improve this resource.</p>

